    in the app.
  </p>
  <div slot="panel">
    <SleepDiaries />
  </div>
</SplitCard>

<div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="sleep-rhythm" /></div>

<!-- ── The short version ── -->
<h3 id="the-short-version" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">The short version</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  How regular your sleep is, not only how long it lasts, is linked with
  survival. In 60,977 UK Biobank adults who wore wrist sensors, how regular
  their sleep was predicted their risk of dying better than how long they
  slept.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  But &ldquo;regular&rdquo; hides four different questions, and each needs a
  different amount of data before it can answer:
</p>
<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Bedtime average:</strong> where the middle of your bedtimes sits.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Drift:</strong> whether that middle is moving.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Spread:</strong> how much your bedtime moves, measured two ways.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Sleep Regularity Index:</strong> whether your whole day, asleep and awake, repeats.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ── Four diaries ── -->
<h3 id="four-diaries" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">Four diaries</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  Picture four people writing down the time they fall asleep, every night
  for two weeks.
</p>
<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Person A is steady.</strong> Asleep within about ten minutes of 23:00, every night.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Person B slides.</strong> Five nights around 23:00, then each night lands 12 to 15 minutes after the last. No single night looks unusual.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Person C is scattered.</strong> 23:00, 02:00, 22:00, 01:00, 23:30. Late and early, with no direction.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Person D sleeps Person A&rsquo;s nights to the minute</strong>, but naps for two hours on some afternoons and not others.</li>
</ul>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">Here is what each measure makes of them:</p>
<div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <table class="w-full min-w-[600px] text-left text-[12.5px]">
    <thead>
      <tr class="border-b border-white/10 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wide text-text-tertiary">
        <th class="pb-2 pr-4 font-medium"></th>
        <th class="pb-2 pr-4 font-medium">Bedtime average, week 1 &rarr; week 2</th>
        <th class="pb-2 pr-4 font-medium">Drift</th>
        <th class="pb-2 pr-4 font-medium">Spread: night to night &middot; around the average</th>
        <th class="pb-2 font-medium">Regularity index</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
      <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Person A, steady</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">23:03 &rarr; 23:03</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">none</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">8 min &middot; &plusmn;5 min</td><td class="py-2 font-mono">91</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Person B, sliding</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">23:08 &rarr; 00:20</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-warning">1 h 12 later</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">11 min &middot; &plusmn;41 min</td><td class="py-2 font-mono">91</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Person C, scattered</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">23:47 &rarr; 23:51</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">4 min</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-warning">2 h 30 &middot; &plusmn;83 min</td><td class="py-2 font-mono text-warning">73</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Person D, naps some days</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">23:03 &rarr; 23:03</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">none</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">8 min &middot; &plusmn;5 min</td><td class="py-2 font-mono text-warning">80</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  No single measure catches all three. Only drift catches Person B&rsquo;s
  slide. Spread and the regularity index both catch Person C. Only the
  regularity index catches Person D. The average alone flags no one.
</p>

<!-- ── 1. Bedtime average ── -->
<h3 id="1-bedtime-average-where-the-middle-is" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">1. Bedtime average: where the middle is</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">It answers <em>what time do I usually fall asleep?</em></p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  Clock times sit on a circle, so they have to be averaged as angles. With
  plain arithmetic, 23:50 and 00:10 are 23 hours 40 minutes apart and
  average to noon. On a clock face they are 20 minutes apart and average
  to midnight.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  The average settles fast. In 10,412 adults who wore a WHOOP band for a
  year, three nights gave a reliable average time of falling asleep, and
  seven a reliable average total sleep time (Leota, <em>Sleep</em> 2026).
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  It can&rsquo;t tell you how consistent you are. Person C&rsquo;s average,
  23:47, looks perfectly ordinary.
</p>

<!-- ── 2. Drift ── -->
<h3 id="2-drift-whether-the-middle-is-moving" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">2. Drift: whether the middle is moving</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  Compare one week&rsquo;s average with the next. Person B&rsquo;s moved
  from 23:08 to 00:20: an hour and 12 minutes later in a single week,
  though no night was more than 15 minutes from the one before.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  Drift is the only one of the four with a direction. It needs two windows
  to compare, so two weeks of nights.
</p>

<!-- ── 3. Spread ── -->
<h3 id="3-spread-how-much-your-bedtime-moves" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">3. Spread: how much your bedtime moves</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  It answers <em>how much does my bedtime bounce around?</em> Verve
  measures it two ways, because each one sees something the other
  doesn&rsquo;t.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">Night to night</strong> is the average size of the move from
  one night to the next, in minutes, whichever way it goes: 8 for Person
  A, 11 for Person B, 2 hours 30 for Person C.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">Around your average</strong> is the standard deviation: how
  widely your bedtimes scatter around their own average. It is one of the
  measures of regularity listed in the American Heart Association&rsquo;s
  2025 statement on sleep health, which gives 60 minutes or less as a
  commonly used cut-point while noting there is no agreement on the best
  threshold (St-Onge, <em>Circ Cardiovasc Qual Outcomes</em> 2025). Person
  A reads &plusmn;5 minutes and Person C &plusmn;83.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  Read the two together. Person B&rsquo;s nightly moves are small, 11
  minutes, but around the average Person B reads &plusmn;41, because
  measured from the middle the slide looks like scatter. When
  around-your-average is much wider than night to night, the gap is drift.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  Both are slow to trust. In the same 10,412 adults, a reliable standard
  deviation took 41 to 65 nights depending on the measure, and 44 for the
  time of falling asleep. Estimates from 7 to 14 nights, the length of most
  sleep studies, were poor, and a single week could misjudge the
  variability of total sleep time by as much as 50 minutes either way
  (Leota, <em>Sleep</em> 2026). No study has measured this for
  night-to-night moves, so Verve applies the same 44 nights to both.
</p>

<!-- ── 4. Sleep Regularity Index ── -->
<h3 id="4-sleep-regularity-index-whether-your-whole-day-repeats" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">4. Sleep Regularity Index: whether your whole day repeats</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  It answers <em>am I asleep and awake at the same clock minutes today as
  yesterday?</em> It checks every minute of the 24 hours against the same
  minute a day earlier, so waking up, naps and time awake in the night all
  count, not just bedtime. 100 is a day that repeats exactly. 0 is no
  better than chance.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  That is why Person D scores 80 to Person A&rsquo;s 91 despite identical
  nights. It is also why Person B scores 91: a slow slide changes each day
  only a little from the day before, and the index compares only
  neighbouring days (Fischer, <em>Sleep</em> 2021).
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  This is the measure the outcome data rest on. Across 60,977 UK Biobank
  adults followed for an average of 6.3 years, the four most regular fifths
  had a 20 to 48 per cent lower risk of death from any cause than the least
  regular fifth, and regularity predicted it better than duration did
  (Windred, <em>Sleep</em> 2024). A second analysis, of 88,975
  participants, found the risk concentrated at the irregular end: a hazard
  ratio of 1.53 at the 5th percentile against 0.90 at the 95th, both
  relative to the median (Cribb, <em>eLife</em> 2023). The extra risk sat
  mostly in the irregular tail.
</p>

<!-- The UK Biobank bands, as literature: the canvas moved them here from
     the app, because a score from a different calculator is context for
     yours, not a scale to put it on. -->
<div class="mt-4 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">
    What the scores looked like in those studies
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 flex h-2 overflow-hidden rounded-full">
    <span class="bg-[rgba(244,63,94,0.35)]" style="width: 71.6%"></span>
    <span class="bg-[rgba(251,191,36,0.45)]" style="width: 15.7%"></span>
    <span class="bg-[rgba(52,211,153,0.4)]" style="width: 12.7%"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="relative h-[13px]">
    <span class="absolute top-0.5 h-[9px] w-0.5 bg-text-primary" style="left: 81%"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="relative h-3.5 font-mono text-[9.5px]">
    <span class="absolute left-0 text-text-muted">0</span>
    <span class="absolute -translate-x-1/2 text-warning" style="left: 71.6%">71.6</span>
    <span class="absolute -translate-x-1/2 text-success-light" style="left: 87.3%">87.3</span>
    <span class="absolute right-0 text-text-muted">100</span>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-3 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    In Windred&rsquo;s sample the median was 81.0, marked, and half the
    sample sat between 73.8 and 86.3. The least regular fifth scored below
    71.6 and the most regular fifth above 87.3. Cribb&rsquo;s analysis of
    UK Biobank put the median at 60.
  </p>
</div>

<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">Why Verve doesn&rsquo;t put your score on that scale.</strong>
  Those two medians come from the same biobank. When two widely used SRI
  calculators were run over the same 73,794 adults, their scores differed
  enough to change what the outcome models concluded (Czeisler,
  <em>Sleep</em> 2026). A score only compares with scores from the same
  calculation, so Verve reads yours against your own past months, and the
  study figures stay here as context.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">It needs time.</strong> It takes at least five to seven days
  of continuous sleep data to calculate. Related regularity scores built
  from fewer than seven days carry measurable bias and don&rsquo;t compare
  with longer recordings (Lok, <em>J Sleep Res</em> 2025). How long one
  person&rsquo;s score takes to settle has no agreed answer yet.
</p>

<!-- ── How many nights ── -->
<h3 id="how-many-nights-each-one-needs" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">How many nights each one needs</h3>
<div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] text-left text-[12.5px]">
    <thead>
      <tr class="border-b border-white/10 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wide text-text-tertiary">
        <th class="pb-2 pr-4 font-medium">Measure</th>
        <th class="pb-2 pr-4 font-medium">Can be calculated from</th>
        <th class="pb-2 font-medium">Worth trusting from</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
      <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Bedtime average</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">2 to 3 nights</td><td class="py-2 font-mono text-success-light">3 nights <span class="text-text-tertiary">(7 for total sleep time)</span></td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Drift</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">two weekly averages</td><td class="py-2 font-mono text-success-light">two weeks</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Spread, both ways</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">about 7 nights</td><td class="py-2 font-mono text-warning">41 to 65 nights</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Sleep Regularity Index</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono">5 to 7 days</td><td class="py-2 font-mono text-text-tertiary">no agreed figure yet</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  So the average can be trusted within days, and drift within a fortnight.
  Spread and the regularity index stay provisional much longer. Verve
  counts both as settled at 44 nights, about six weeks; for the regularity
  index, where there is no agreed figure, that is Verve&rsquo;s own choice.
</p>

<!-- ── What these measures are not ── -->
<h3 id="what-these-measures-are-not" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">What these measures are not</h3>
<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Not hours.</strong> A steady six-hour sleeper can look more regular than an erratic eight-hour one. Timing and duration are separate markers, each linked with health in its own right.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Not sleep quality.</strong> The regularity index sees only asleep or awake, not how deep the sleep was.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Not travel-proof.</strong> They compare clock times, so crossing time zones reads as irregular even when your body&rsquo;s rhythm is steady.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ── In Verve ── -->
<h3 id="in-verve" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-9 text-[17px] font-semibold text-text-primary">In Verve</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  Verve reads the sleep your watch or ring writes to Apple Health and works
  out all four measures the way this page describes.
</p>
<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
  <li>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">The Sleep page</strong> keeps to numbers, under three
    headings. <em>Bedtime average with drift</em>: your average bedtime
    over the last seven nights, and how far it moved from the seven before,
    in amber once the move passes 20 minutes. <em>Bedtime spread</em>:
    night to night, and around your average, in green or amber against the
    60-minute mark. <em>Sleep regularity index (SRI)</em>: the last 30
    days, beside your own median. Until 44 nights are in, spread and the
    index stay grey with a count toward 44.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Trends</strong> shows the same three cards. The Snapshot
    sets this week&rsquo;s average bedtime against last week&rsquo;s, with
    spread and the index as on the Sleep page. Over Time shows all three
    for the period you pick, each with a thin line of how its number moved:
    night by night, week by week, month by month or year by year. On the
    spread and index lines, points resting on fewer than 44 nights are
    hollow; on the months view, the index line also carries your median for
    the year.
  </li>
</ul>

<!-- ── Take-home ── -->
<div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-accent/25 bg-accent/[0.06] p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent-light">Take-home</p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
    Start with the two you can trust soonest: your average bedtime, within
    a few nights, and whether it is drifting, within two weeks. Give the
    spread and the regularity index about six weeks, then read them against
    yourself.
  </p>
</div>

<div id="connect-wearables" class="mt-4 scroll-mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Connecting Oura, Whoop or Garmin.</strong>
    Verve reads only Apple Health, so a third-party wearable reaches it
    through its own app&rsquo;s Apple Health setting. <em>Oura</em>: the
    Oura app &rarr; menu &rarr; Settings &rarr; Apple Health, and allow it
    to write Sleep, Workouts, Heart Rate and Respiratory Rate.
    <em>Whoop</em>: More &rarr; App Settings &rarr; Integrations &rarr;
    Apple Health, and allow the same categories. <em>Garmin</em>: Garmin
    Connect &rarr; More &rarr; Settings &rarr; Apple Health. Then in Verve,
    Settings &rarr; Apple Health on. How far back an app writes when you
    switch the integration on varies by app; from that day on every night
    arrives. Stages appear when the app writes them; a plain asleep record
    shows as one block. Steps come from whatever writes them to Health:
    the phone in a pocket, an Apple Watch, and Oura or Garmin through
    their apps; Whoop writes a day&rsquo;s steps as one block the morning
    after, which Health drops beside the phone&rsquo;s own samples (see the
    Whoop section below). In Verve, Settings &rarr;
    Wearables shows which app wrote the latest sleep, heart-rate and
    workout sample, and when. Wearing a Whoop? The step-by-step is
    <a href="#whoop" class="text-accent-light underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-2">below</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">More than one wearable.</strong>
    An Apple Watch writes to Health directly; Oura, Whoop and Garmin write
    through their apps. Someone with a Watch and a Whoop therefore has two
    writers, and Health keeps both copies. Who decides which one counts
    depends on the reading, and there are three cases.
    <em>Verve chooses, and you can set it</em>: sleep stages, night HRV,
    resting heart rate, VO&#8322;max and, from the next update, workouts. Health hands over every
    writer&rsquo;s samples with no order attached, so Verve picks one writer
    per night or day and names it on the card. By default the device that
    covered more of the night, or wrote more readings; in Verve, Settings
    &rarr; Apple Health &rarr; Preferred device, you can name the one that
    leads for each of the four. The other still counts on the nights it is
    alone.
    <em>Health decides, by its source order</em>: steps, active energy and
    distance. Verve asks Health for the day&rsquo;s total, and Health builds
    it before answering, keeping one source for any minute both counted.
    The order is yours to set: Health &rarr; Browse &rarr; Steps &rarr;
    Data Sources &amp; Access &rarr; Edit &rarr; drag the device you trust
    to the top. Verve never sees the pieces, so it cannot offer a choice.
    <em>Workouts</em> are events with a start time, and two devices
    recording the same run leave two records a minute apart. Verve treats
    two records from different writers that cover most of the same minutes
    as one session and keeps one: the device you named, else the one that
    carried heart rate, else the longer. A session only one device saw is
    untouched, and nothing is deleted from Health.
    <em>Each record on its own</em>: weight and blood pressure, where the
    newest reading or each cuff reading simply stands. For any reading, the
    per-app switch in Health, Sharing &rarr; Apps &rarr; the app &rarr; the
    type, stops a second copy at the source; the Apple Watch, being a
    device rather than an app, has no such switches.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- ── WHOOP, step by step ── -->
<div id="whoop" class="mt-4 scroll-mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent-light">If you wear a Whoop</p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    A Whoop band reaches Verve the same way every third-party wearable
    does: the band talks to the Whoop app, the Whoop app writes to Apple
    Health, and Verve reads Apple Health. Pairing the band is not the
    connection; the two switches below are. What follows was checked on a
    Whoop paired on 25 October 2026: Health&rsquo;s own permission list for
    the app, then what the first night actually wrote.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">1. Let Whoop write to Health.</strong>
    In the Whoop app: More &rarr; Integrations &rarr; Apple Health, and
    follow it through. Once connected, Apple Health sits above the
    &ldquo;Not connected&rdquo; line on that Integrations screen.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">2. Check the write switches, not the read ones.</strong>
    iPhone Health app &rarr; your picture &rarr; Apps &rarr; WHOOP. The
    page has two lists. <em>Allow &ldquo;WHOOP&rdquo; to write</em> is the
    one that matters: every switch on. Below it, <em>Allow
    &ldquo;WHOOP&rdquo; to read</em> is a long list (vitamins, caffeine,
    insulin, blood glucose) of what Whoop may pull <em>out</em> of Health
    for its own app; Verve never uses any of it, and it is fine either way.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">3. Let Verve read Health.</strong>
    In Verve, Settings &rarr; Apple Health, on. Then Health &rarr; Apps
    &rarr; Verve, every read switch on. Nothing else in Verve&rsquo;s
    settings depends on which band you wear.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">4. Wait for the first night.</strong>
    Whoop writes after it has processed something: a night&rsquo;s sleep or
    a workout. Until then Health&rsquo;s &ldquo;Data from WHOOP&rdquo; says
    no data found, and Verve lists no Whoop anywhere, because it only names
    a device that has actually written in the last 14 days. Next morning,
    open the Whoop app first and let it finish the night, then open Verve.
    Settings &rarr; Wearables should name Whoop on the sleep and heart-rate
    rows, with the time it wrote.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">5. If you also had an Apple Watch.</strong>
    Settings &rarr; Apple Health &rarr; Preferred device. Once Whoop has
    written, each row offers it; set sleep stages, resting heart rate and
    workouts to Whoop. This matters even with the Watch in a drawer: for two
    weeks the Watch still has more readings in Health&rsquo;s window than
    the Whoop, and the automatic rule follows readings.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What Whoop may write to Health</strong>
    (Health&rsquo;s own write list for the app, October 2026): active
    energy, blood oxygen, heart rate, respiratory rate, resting heart rate,
    sleep, steps, workouts.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What the first night actually wrote</strong>
    (Health &rarr; Apps &rarr; WHOOP &rarr; Data from WHOOP, the morning of
    26 October): steps, resting heart rate, sleep, blood oxygen, respiratory
    rate. Sleep arrived as one asleep block from 21:30 to 06:24 with gaps for
    the wake-ups; the Whoop app showed light, deep and REM for the same
    night, but Health did not get them. No heart rate was written overnight,
    switch on or not. Verve read the night as 8h 30, the Whoop app&rsquo;s
    own figure. The first session the same morning, a 31-minute walk
    started in the Whoop app, added Workouts, Heart Rate and Active Energy
    to that list, with the heart rate written only for the session&rsquo;s
    minutes.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What it does not write:</strong>
    HRV, cardio fitness (VO&#8322;max), one-minute heart rate recovery,
    standing time. Whoop keeps HRV and its recovery score inside its own
    app. Verve can only read what a vendor writes.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What that means on each Verve page.</strong>
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Sleep</strong>: full. Every night arrives as one asleep block, with wake-ups as gaps; the stage split stays in the Whoop app. Bedtime average, drift, spread and the regularity index all work, since none of them needs stages.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Resting heart rate</strong>: full, read against your own 30-day median. A Whoop&rsquo;s resting rate and a Watch&rsquo;s are different quantities, so Verve never averages the two; if you switched from a Watch, the &ldquo;usual&rdquo; line blends both devices until the Watch days age out, about 30 days.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Workouts</strong>: full, seen on the first session (a 31-minute walk, 26 October). Whoop writes the workout and the heart rate inside it, several samples a minute, about 15 to 20 minutes after the session ends; Verve classifies the minutes from that heart rate. Outside a session Whoop writes no heart rate at all, so a walk you did not start in the Whoop app reaches Verve as steps only.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Steps and movement</strong>: from the phone, not the band. Whoop writes a whole day&rsquo;s steps as one block, the morning after (2,523 steps stamped from midnight on the 24th to bedtime on the 25th, added at 06:52 on the 26th). Health settles two sources whole sample against whole sample, so with iPhone above WHOOP in Health&rsquo;s source order that block is dropped wherever the phone wrote a sample inside it, which is every day you carry the phone; with WHOOP above iPhone the block wins and the phone&rsquo;s minute-by-minute samples inside it are dropped instead, and Verve&rsquo;s sitting hours and cadence, which read steps in 5-minute buckets, see a day smeared flat. Keep iPhone above WHOOP and carry the phone. Standing minutes are an Apple Watch reading and go blank.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Night HRV</strong>: blank. Not written. The Today tile shows a dash with &ldquo;No HRV asleep&rdquo;; the Night HRV page keeps every night another device wrote. The number lives in the Whoop app.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Recovery (one-minute heart rate recovery)</strong>: blank. An Apple Watch reading; the tile shows a dash once the last Watch reading is older than yesterday. Whoop&rsquo;s &ldquo;Recovery&rdquo; is a different thing: a score, not a heart-rate drop, and it stays in the Whoop app.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-text-primary">VO&#8322;max</strong>: none from Whoop. If a Watch wrote one earlier, Verve keeps showing that estimate for up to a year. The fix is the one Verve prefers anyway: run a field test and let the measured number lead.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">The whole picture, in one table.</strong>
    Whoop instead of an Apple Watch, reading by reading: who gives it to
    Verve, and when.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/20">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[620px] text-[12.5px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Reading</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Comes from</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">When, and what to know</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.06]">
        {whoopReadings.map(([r, from, note]) => (
          <tr class="align-top">
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">{r}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-mono text-text-secondary">{from}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">{note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    In one sentence: with a Whoop you keep sleep, resting heart rate and
    started workouts; the phone in your pocket carries live steps, cadence
    and sitting; and you do without night HRV, one-minute heart rate
    recovery, standing minutes and a wearable VO&#8322;max. That last group
    is the real trade of leaving the Watch, and no setting changes it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    Settled on 26 October, from the first night and the first session: sleep
    is one asleep block, not stages; heart rate is written inside a started
    workout and nowhere else; the workout itself lands about 15 to 20
    minutes after it ends; steps arrive as one block the morning after.
    Nothing on this page is from the permission list alone. The
    &ldquo;Steps total&rdquo; line is in Verve from the same day: Steps
    &rarr; Day, under the yesterday strip, &ldquo;WHOOP &middot; 2,523
    &middot; yesterday&rdquo;, read from the band&rsquo;s own samples
    whatever Health&rsquo;s source order says.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- ── WHOOP on Android, and iPhone against Android ── -->
<div id="whoop-android" class="mt-4 scroll-mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent-light">If you wear a Whoop on Android</p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    On Android the store is <strong class="text-text-primary">Health Connect</strong>,
    built into Android 14 and newer, and the path is the same shape: the band
    talks to the Whoop app, the Whoop app writes to Health Connect, and Verve
    reads Health Connect. What follows was checked on a Motorola Edge 60 with a
    Whoop, the evening of 27 October 2026, against Health Connect&rsquo;s own
    permission list for the app and what reached Verve the same evening.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">1. Let Whoop write to Health Connect.</strong>
    In the Whoop app: More &rarr; Integrations &rarr; Health Connect, and allow
    everything it asks for.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">2. Check the write list.</strong>
    Phone Settings &rarr; search &ldquo;Health Connect&rdquo; &rarr; App
    permissions &rarr; WHOOP. The list has a read half and a write half;
    <em>Allowed to write</em> is the one that matters, every switch on.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">3. Let Verve read Health Connect.</strong>
    In Verve, Settings &rarr; Health Connect, on, and allow the permissions it
    asks for. Settings &rarr; Health Connect &rarr; Preferred device then names
    Whoop on the sleep, resting heart rate and workout rows once it has
    written.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">4. One account, both phones.</strong>
    Verve&rsquo;s account is the same on iPhone and Android. What you type and
    what the app keeps for you, tests, weekly totals, labs, the Guide, come
    down to the second phone on sign-in. What a store holds is read on the
    phone that holds it: Apple Health on the iPhone, Health Connect on
    Android. An account made with Sign in with Apple signs in on Android by
    email, with a password set once from &ldquo;Forgot password&rdquo;.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What Whoop may write to Health Connect</strong>
    (Health Connect&rsquo;s own write list for the app, 27 October 2026):
    active calories, exercise, heart rate, lean body mass, oxygen saturation,
    respiratory rate, resting heart rate, sleep, steps, weight.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What it does not write on Android:</strong>
    HRV, distance, basal (resting) energy, cardio fitness (VO&#8322;max),
    height. The same as on the iPhone, with distance and basal energy added:
    Health Connect has a record for each, and Whoop writes neither.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What the first evening wrote:</strong>
    the night&rsquo;s sleep (Verve read 6h 48, the Whoop app&rsquo;s own
    figure), resting heart rate, and the week&rsquo;s two started sessions
    with their heart rate, so the dashboard showed Zone 2 minutes and the
    weekly dose the same evening. No steps yet: as on the iPhone, Whoop
    writes a day&rsquo;s steps as one block the morning after.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">iPhone against Android, reading by reading.</strong>
    &ldquo;From the first&rdquo; means the card fills from the first night or
    session. The waits are Verve&rsquo;s own settling rules and are the same on
    both phones; they count nights or days the store actually holds, so a
    band worn from today starts the count today.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/[0.08]">
    <table class="w-full text-xs">
      <thead class="bg-white/[0.04]">
        <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Reading</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">iPhone + Whoop</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Android + Whoop</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">How long until it shows</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.06]">
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Workouts, Zone 2, weekly dose</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes, with heart rate inside the session</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes, the same</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">From the first session, about 15 to 20 minutes after it ends</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Sleep duration, bedtime</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes, one asleep block a night</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes, the same</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">From the first night; the bedtime average after 3 nights</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Sleep stages</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">No: Whoop keeps the split in its app</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">No, the same</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">Never from Whoop; Oura, Garmin, Pixel Watch and Galaxy Watch write them</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Bedtime spread, sleep regularity</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">A first reading after 7 nights, trustworthy at 44</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Resting heart rate, against your own baseline</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">The number from the first night; the baseline after 14 nights, full at 30</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Night HRV</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">No: Whoop does not write it</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">No, the same</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">Never from Whoop. From a band that writes it: 7 nights, baseline at 30. Apple Health carries SDNN, Health Connect rMSSD; Verve names which and never mixes them</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">One-minute heart rate recovery</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Derived from the heart rate after a started session</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Derived the same way</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">From the first session that ends with the band still reading; the baseline after 5 readings over 30 days</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Steps total</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes, one block the morning after</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes, the same</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">Yesterday from the next morning; today only from a phone or watch counting through the day</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Step cadence, sitting hours</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">From the phone in a pocket, not the band</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">From a phone or watch that writes steps by the minute; Whoop&rsquo;s one block a day is not enough</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">The same day, when such a writer exists</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Physical activity level</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes, estimated from your profile when no basal energy is written</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Not yet: needs a basal energy writer, which Whoop is not</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">After 3 days of active energy on the iPhone</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Weight, lean body mass</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Each reading as written</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">The same</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">As soon as a scale or the app writes one</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Breathing rate, blood oxygen in sleep</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Yes</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">From the first night</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Wearable VO&#8322;max</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">No</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">No</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">Never from Whoop; a typed fitness test shows at once on both</td></tr>
        <tr class="align-top"><td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">Standing minutes</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">Apple Watch only</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">No Android store carries it</td><td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">Never from Whoop on either</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    In one sentence: a Whoop gives the same Verve on either phone, sleep,
    resting heart rate and started workouts, and leaves the same gaps, night
    HRV, stages, a wearable VO&#8322;max; the one difference today is the
    physical activity level, which the Android app does not yet estimate
    without a basal energy writer.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    Settled on 27 October from the first evening on Android: the night and
    the week&rsquo;s two sessions arrived within minutes of switching the
    integration on; steps had not. The permission list is Health Connect&rsquo;s
    own; the rest is what Verve showed.
  </p>
</div>
